    "md5": "1dfb316ae979a77c97d21c7e2a5ac56f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1388,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka is not a homogenous society. It is plural, layered and rich with many traditions, beliefs and identities. It was only in 1835 the entire country came under one rule and as one country by the British. A mandate for system change does not mean that those who won the election are its only guardians. It does not entitle them to decide by themselves what that change should look like, nor to act without listening to those who must live with the consequences. As the government enters its second year, it needs to recognise that democracy is not only about the mandate received at election time. This mandate can be lost as former President Gotabaya Rajapaksa and his government experienced in 2022.\n\nThese concerns, whether valid or not, would have been better addressed if the government had presented its case for the educational reforms before announcing the reforms. In a sense, the government created the conditions for the opposition to the reforms to consolidate. It did not circulate or discuss the reforms with the teachers, academics, religious leaders or parents who would be directly affected. No preliminary consultations were held with the opposition or with civil society. What was presented could instead have been a draft for discussion and revision. Had the government engaged in open discussion at an early stage, many misunderstandings might have been avoided."
