    "chunk_text": "Health sector ills\n\nThe state health sector is perennially ill, and the people are sick of it, so to speak. Nary a day passes without a strike or a protest staged by one category of health workers or another. Doctors are up in arms again. The Government Medical Officers\u2019 Association (GMOA) has been complaining of irregularities in doctors\u2019 transfers due to administrative or political interference. It decided to stage a countrywide token strike yesterday in protest against an alleged government move to implement doctors\u2019 transfers arbitrarily, but it walked back its decision, following a discussion with Minister of Health Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa and some Healthy Ministry officials on Thursday night.\n\nThese are hard times. Most people are struggling to make ends meet, and cannot afford private healthcare. They are dependent on state-run hospitals, which are experiencing drug shortages and inordinate delays due to resource constraints. Complaints abound that patients who visit government hospitals have to buy medicines from private pharmacies. A doctors\u2019 strike will make their plight even more intolerable.\n\nGovernments refuse to grant doctors\u2019 demands and opt for a game of chicken, driving the GMOA to strike or threaten to do so, and eventually politicians and bureaucrats give in after causing untold suffering to patients. The current dispensation is no exception. It is moving along the same rut as its predecessors in handling strikes in the health sector and elsewhere. Thankfully, sanity prevailed at long last, and the government swallowed its pride and opted to have a discussion with the protesting doctors."
  },
