    "md5": "64e9479e063563b68fd4b59ed23ff7a1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "However, shouldn\u2019t institutions like courts be located away from residential areas for security and spatial reasons? There have been shooting incidents in and around courts. It has been revealed that underworld figure Keselbaddara Padme (Padmasiri Perera), who is currently being detained, planned to kill one of his rivals, Harak Kata (Nadun Wickremeratne), by attacking a prison bus with a claymore mine.\n\nWhile the government is in overdrive to establish more courts, pressure is mounting on it to abandon what the Opposition describes as a sinister plan to close down low-enrollment schools. The government has denied the charge, but President Anura Kumara Dissanayake argued for closing such schools permanently while waxing eloquent on the government\u2019s educational reforms in Parliament in July 2025. He said, inter alia, that about 15% schools had fewer than 50 students each, and about two-thirds of schools had fewer than 100 students each, and a strategy to overcome the problem might necessitate permanent closure of some of those seats of learning\n\nThe Ceylon Teachers\u2019 Union (CTU) is on the warpath, threatening a strike unless the government walks back its decision to go ahead with the proposed educational reforms. It says eight small schools have been closed in the North Central Province. CTU President Priyantha Fernando has said at a recent CTU event in Anuradhapura District that the incumbent government, which claims to espouse socialism, has done something that not even its predecessors described as agents of neoliberalism did. It says that as many as 1,500 small schools have been earmarked for closure, and downsizing the teacher service is one of the main conditions for a foreign loan amounting to USD 200 million. The government has not responded to this allegation."
  },
