    "chunk_size_bytes": 1871,
    "chunk_text": "Thankfully, a few days later, the Gampaha Additional Magistrate directed the Yakkala Police to evict all those who were forcibly occupying the FSP office and keep the premises under police custody until the Gampaha District Court delivered a ruling on the ownership of the place. Thus, it became obvious that the JVP activists had told the police a blatant lie and caused an affront to the dignity of the judiciary. But the police did not take any action against those who had misled them. The JVP cadres\u2019 false claim at issue, in our book, is far more serious than pretending to be a sibling of an SDIG.\n\nThe Opposition has also accused the police of having refrained from arresting a deputy minister and another NPP MP facing a fraud charge although they swiftly arrest the political opponents of the government for lesser offences. As we pointed out in a previous editorial comment, those who expected the 2024 regime change to bring about a radical break from the rotten political culture that flourished under previous governments must be disappointed and disillusioned.\n\nOn 01 May 2025, scores of JVP supporters on their way to the NPP\u2019s International Labour Day rally at Galle Face broke journey, with their buses unlawfully parked on the Southern Expressway. They leisurely had lunch and strolled along the expressway. The police looked the other way. They would have arrested the culprits and hauled them up before courts in double-quick time if the latter had not been government supporters.\n\nThe NPP\u2019s rule began with a false claim. In 2024, the then Speaker of Parliament Asoka Ranwala claimed to have obtained a doctorate from a Japanese university. The government insisted that his claim was true, but when pressure mounted on it to furnish proof of his educational qualifications, it threw him under the bus. A fish is said to rot from the head down."
